Deduplicated point-instancer prototypes keep their visibility primvars

With the default geometry deduplication (HDARNOLD_GEOM_DEDUP=1, Instances), identical
point-instancer prototypes are rendered from a single canonical node, and each duplicate's
instancer overrides the visibility of its instances. A duplicate skipped its primvars, so its
visibility ignored primvars:arnold:visibility (full mask) and primvars:arnold:visibility:<ray>,
and its instances rendered even when its prototype was hidden from the camera.

The scene has a mesh and a curves point instancer, each with two identical prototypes hidden
from the camera (deduplicated) and a slightly different visible prototype. The meshes are hidden
with primvars:arnold:visibility:camera, the curves with the full primvars:arnold:visibility
mask. Only the visible prototypes must render. The deduplication only exists in the render
delegate, so the test is skipped for the usd pass (see pass_usd in testsuite/groups), which
also ignores the visibility primvars of point-instancer prototypes.

author: sebastien.blaineau.ortega@autodesk.com

PARAMS: {'scene':'test.usda'}
